## Analysis

The failing test `tree-subfailure` with args `['-r', 'tree', 'dst']` expects stderr to be nonempty when a sub-operation within a recursive tree copy fails. 

The root cause: in `copy_tree`, when a child `copy_path`, `copy_tree`, or `recreate_symlink` call fails (returns -1), the function silently sets `rc = 1` without emitting any diagnostic to stderr. The main function only prints a diagnostic when `copy_tree` itself returns -1 (startup failure), but for sub-failures it returns 1, so nothing is printed.

## Fix

Add error diagnostics in `copy_tree` for each failed child operation. When a child returns -1 (actual error), print a diagnostic naming the failed child path. When it returns 1 (interactive decline or deeper sub-failure that already printed), don't double-print.

